<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">WA Mandatory Resale Safety Code Compliance</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Navigating Washington State's mandatory statutory retrofits for real estate sales: water heater seismic double-strapping, carbon monoxide alarms, smoke detector compliance, and passing FHA/VA appraisals.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Why Low-Cost Safety Retrofits Protect Closing Timelines</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Here is the good news for both buyers and sellers: Washington State's statutory resale safety requirements are inexpensive ($20 to $30 per item) and take less than 30 minutes to complete. However, mortgage appraisers (especially for FHA and VA loans) will immediately flag missing water heater straps or missing carbon monoxide detectors as mandatory repair conditions, halting closing until verified. 
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        Sellers should knock these quick DIY items out <strong>before taking listing photos</strong> to avoid petty inspection repair demands or appraisal holdups. For buyers, missing straps or detectors are minor 15-minute handyman fixes that shouldn't derail a great offer.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The Big Three Statutory Requirements for WA Real Estate Sales</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Under Washington State law, every single-family home, townhome, or condominium transferred must satisfy three specific life-safety requirements before the buyer or new occupant takes possession.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">RCW 19.27.520</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Water Heater Seismic Strapping</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Tank water heaters must be double-strapped (upper 1/3 and lower 1/3 of tank) and anchored securely into structural wall studs.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">RCW 19.27.530</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Carbon Monoxide (CO) Alarms</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Required outside of each sleeping area, in immediate bedroom vicinities, and on every story of the residence to detect invisible, odorless combustion gas.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">RCW 43.44.110</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Smoke Detection Devices</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Required inside every bedroom, outside sleeping hallways, and on every story of the residence.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">DIY Water Heater Strapping Guide &amp; Reference</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Because King and Snohomish counties sit in a seismic zone, unstrapped water heaters pose a major fire and flooding hazard during earthquakes. Installing seismic straps is a fast, low-cost DIY project that satisfies state code and clears appraiser checklists.
    </p>

    <div class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card" style="padding: 1.5rem;">
      <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
        
        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Phase 01: Locate Wall Studs</span>
          <p class="text-body">Use a stud finder to locate framing studs on both sides of the tank. (If studs are misaligned, screw a horizontal 2x4 cross-brace into the studs first).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Phase 02: Position Dual Straps</span>
          <p class="text-body">Measure the top third and bottom third of the tank height. Keep the lower strap at least 4 inches above the lower gas/electric controls to prevent blockage.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Phase 03: Anchor Lag Screws</span>
          <p class="text-body">Fasten heavy-duty metal belt straps into the wall studs using 1/4" x 3" lag screws with washers. (Plumber's tape is strictly prohibited for strapping).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Phase 04: Tighten Tension Belts</span>
          <p class="text-body">Wrap belts around the front of the tank and tighten the cinch bolts until the tank is firmly immobilized against the structural wall.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="action-tray-constrained section-action-tray" style="width: 100%; max-width: 100%;">
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MHJGM5e72EU" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-secondary">
          <span>📺 How to Strap a Water Heater (YouTube Video Tutorial) &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Alarm Placement &amp; Hardware Specifications</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Correctly placing functional environmental alarms is the final step in ensuring property transfer compliance.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Carbon Monoxide (CO) Alarms</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Hardware Specifications</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Produced by fuel-burning appliances, gas furnaces, or fireplaces, CO is deadly and undetectable. RCW 19.27.530 allows for battery-powered, wall plug-in with battery backup, or hardwired units. Combination smoke/CO alarms are also fully compliant.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Smoke Detection Devices</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Hardware Specifications</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Required in all sleep areas and hallways. While standard battery detectors satisfy state code, upgrading to sealed 10-year lithium battery units is highly recommended to eliminate low-battery chirping and maintenance hassles for incoming buyers.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

